Follow a chemical through terminals and intermodal handoffs (@cs-intermodal: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @chemical-supply-systems, #source "../sources/Cargo-Transport-Units-Code.lingua#r_4YS4SQPG6WKX5TWAF48M0VV9X1", #source "../sources/Methanol-Safe-Handling-Manual.lingua#r_01VD6M07PMPE9J0W00Z9KD5WC0", #source "../sources/Dangerous-Goods-Model-Regulations-UN.lingua#r_608ADXFZHPBDY8MYGJZ9M1BG06", #source "../sources/Graduate-Logistics-Systems-MIT.lingua#r_4NMCCS7JPNK6CD9V9Z4XTQPKR8", #chemical-supply-systems "Point-of-use-conditioning-filtration-dilution-and-release.lingua#r_7B2CJQGS5NV8WKM09952PT3VHC") { r_2J9HM9CW5PTVNFS003X1KRNT98
**Read:** [Code of Practice for Packing of Cargo Transport Units — IMO, ILO and UNECE](../sources/Cargo-Transport-Units-Code.lingua#r_4YS4SQPG6WKX5TWAF48M0VV9X1), the entire code and all applicable annexes; [Methanol Safe Handling Manual — Methanol Institute](../sources/Methanol-Safe-Handling-Manual.lingua#r_01VD6M07PMPE9J0W00Z9KD5WC0), the complete marine transport, terminals and storage passages; [Recommendations on the Transport of Dangerous Goods — UN Model Regulations](../sources/Dangerous-Goods-Model-Regulations-UN.lingua#r_608ADXFZHPBDY8MYGJZ9M1BG06), the complete applicable multimodal packaging/tank and documentation provisions; [Graduate Logistics Systems — MIT ESD.260J](../sources/Graduate-Logistics-Systems-MIT.lingua#r_4NMCCS7JPNK6CD9V9Z4XTQPKR8), the complete transport and network-design material.

Draw a complete intermodal route from producer to user with each terminal, vehicle, package or cargo unit, inventory period and responsible party. A route is not just a line on a map: equipment compatibility, receiving slots, documentation, weather, transloading, condition preservation and returns can determine its usable capacity and time.

Read the full CTU Code and applicable material-specific transport requirements, keeping separate its informative material. Distinguish a packaged chemical in a cargo unit from bulk liquid or gas carriage; they do not share every physical or regulatory method. Follow the methanol manual's marine/terminal case as a detailed product-specific companion, while identifying the limits of its date and scope.

Develop a terminal inventory and queue account with actual or clearly synthetic observations. Trace one delayed or damaged consignment and identify who has the evidence needed to decide its disposition. Compare a direct route and one with intermediate storage on usable material, service and dependence. Investigate whether nominally different carriers share the same port, tank farm, customs/information system or contact equipment. The later continuity study will test these common dependencies explicitly.

**Continue:** [Explain what changes between delivered material and usable feed](Point-of-use-conditioning-filtration-dilution-and-release.lingua#r_7B2CJQGS5NV8WKM09952PT3VHC).

